@startuml 04-state-health
!include _style.puml
title Virtual Weather Station - Sensor health state machine (SensorHealthMonitor)

left to right direction

[*] --> OK

OK         : usable() == true
SUSPECT    : usable() == true
RECOVERING : usable() == true\n(on probation)
FAULTY     : usable() == false\ncontributes to no estimate

OK --> SUSPECT : rejection run >= 3\nor silent >= 6 sample periods\nor > 10% of last 64 rejected
OK --> FAULTY  : 12 identical readings,\nnot at a range limit  <<stuck>>\nor > 25% of last 64 rejected

SUSPECT --> FAULTY : rejection run >= 8\nor silent >= 20 sample periods\nor > 25% of last 64 rejected
SUSPECT --> OK     : clean run >= 10

FAULTY     --> RECOVERING : clean run >= 10
RECOVERING --> OK         : clean run >= 25
RECOVERING --> FAULTY     : any rejection\nduring probation

legend bottom
  Only FAULTY is excluded from fusion. SUSPECT means "watch this one": the variance
  estimate has already cut the weight of a sensor misbehaving enough to matter.

  Every threshold counts an uninterrupted **run**. One accepted reading clears the
  rejection counter and vice versa, and a transition clears neither, so "clean run >= 25"
  is the total length needed to confirm a recovery, not a further 25 on top of the 10.

  The rate rule exists because the run rules have a blind spot. A sensor spiking 40% of
  the time rejects about a third of its samples and is plainly broken, but eight rejections
  in a row has probability 0.4^8 - about 0.4 occurrences in 600 samples - so it merely
  oscillated between OK and SUSPECT. The rate rule only escalates; recovery stays with
  the runs.
end legend
@enduml
